\section{Project and bundle objects}{

A \code{gRm_project} contains \code{paths}, ordered \code{variables}, \code{items},
\code{backgrounds}, \code{raw_data}, \code{category_levels}, import metadata, and source
trace information. The variable tables retain source label codes, raw-data
positions, declared category counts, ordinal source types, names, and roles.
\code{raw_data} is the encoded, one-based source table in declared variable
order. A computational bundle contains \code{model}, \code{manifest}, and \code{data}.
Its item and background matrices are zero-based; its rows remain aligned
with project rows; and \code{data$status == 1L} is the conditional-likelihood
estimation sample. Diagnostic routines use their explicitly named record
policies rather than assuming that every diagnostic shares this mask.
}

\section{Analysis object}{

A \code{gRm_analysis} owns the original \code{data}, its encoded \code{project}, ordered
item/exogenous names, ID declaration, and normalized \code{score_groups}.
\code{analysis_identity} is the canonical schema-versioned payload covering
roles, score cuts, category maps, row order and identity, encoded values,
and missing/validity state. \code{analysis_fingerprint} identifies that exact
payload. \code{likelihood_sample} stores its own schema, fingerprint, logical row
mask, indices, and count. Constructors for downstream objects must carry
these fields unchanged; fitting rejects an analysis mutated after creation.
}

\section{Model and SCREEN objects}{

A \code{gRm_model} owns its \code{analysis}, canonical source-ordered \code{ld} and \code{dif}
tables, the same tables under \code{terms}, a \code{model_type} (\code{"rasch"} or
\code{"gllrm"}), optional originating SCREEN object, trace/status fields, and the
unchanged analysis fingerprint and likelihood sample. A \code{gRm_screen} owns
the same analysis identity fields plus the complete source-shaped SCREEN
\code{values}, normalized exact-command state, and final canonical terms.
Provisional evidence rows are retained in \code{values}; only finalized selected
rows may appear in \code{model_terms()} or a model created from SCREEN.
}

\section{Fit object}{

A \code{gRm_fit} owns the exact input \code{analysis} and \code{model} (also available as
transition alias \code{spec}), the source-shaped computational \code{bundle}, the
low-level attempted \code{fit} state, public numeric \code{values} (also available as
transition alias \code{parameters}), a \code{gRm_convergence_state}, trace/status
fields, and the unchanged identity fields. The positive conditional
objective in \code{values} is DIGRAM's negative log likelihood; only
\code{logLik.gRm_fit()} changes its sign to R's log-likelihood convention.
Likelihood comparisons require identical canonical analyses and likelihood
samples and consecutive models whose complete LD/DIF term sets are nested.
}

\section{Public convergence state}{

\code{gRm_fit$convergence} is a \code{gRm_convergence_state} with schema identifier,
final and pre-post-acceptance source flags, iteration count, source stopping
\code{report_delta}, recomputed \code{final_delta}, strict \code{tolerance}, \code{max_step},
stable \code{stop_reason}, post-stop-acceptance flag, attempted-fit metadata, and
\code{report_value_source}. These deltas are maximum sufficient-count
discrepancies, not parameter movements. \code{delta} and \code{max_delta} are
transition aliases for \code{report_delta} and \code{tolerance}.
}

\section{Value and diagnostic result objects}{

Numerical value helpers return source-shaped named lists or tables with
native-precision statistics and source-order rows. Fit values include item
labels, gamma parameters, thresholds, locations, ICE/MICE effects, item
statistics, observed score range, non-negative integer parameter count, and
the finite positive negative log likelihood. A diagnostic result class such
as \code{gRm_local_dependence}, \code{gRm_dif}, \code{gRm_global_homogeneity}, \code{gRm_cm2}, or
\code{gRm_cm3} owns \code{analysis}, the supplied \code{fit}, raw \code{values}, a stable \code{result}
label, method metadata, trace/status fields, and unchanged identity fields.
Public diagnostics compute from the supplied fit and do not silently refit.
}

\section{Summary objects}{

Every package summary is a list inheriting first from
\verb{summary.<input-class>}, then \code{summary.gRm}. It contains \code{title}, selected
\code{which} sections, a named \code{tables} list, printable headers and remarks,
display flags, and the input \code{object_class}; selected tables are also exposed
by their section names for programmatic access. Attributes may retain
statistic-family metadata such as BH thresholds or source status. Printing
formats this object but does not alter the native numeric tables.
}

\section{Validation objects and artifacts}{

The lightweight \code{validation} field on analysis/model objects is status
metadata only; it is not oracle evidence. Repository validation lives under
\verb{validation/} and persists a schema-versioned run manifest before numerical
work, then raw R result objects, extracted value tables, rendered reports,
normalized oracle and R tables, complete comparisons, summaries, timings,
failure subsets, and an event log. Comparisons consume persisted values, and
failed runs retain every artifact completed before failure. Production
package computations never read these artifacts or invoke Pascal.
}

